[web/changes/organizer-fee-paid-checkbox-20261005/05-security-delta.md#F3CE]
1:# Security Delta - organizer-fee-paid-checkbox-20261005
2:
3:## Trust boundary
4:
5:The organizer UI is authenticated but not trusted for anything financial. Every
6:decision that could move money is taken server-side.
7:
8:## What was added
9:
10:A new organizer-reachable write path: `tournament_participants.is_paid`.
11:
12:## Controls
13:
14:| Control | Where | Why |
15:|---|---|---|
16:| Authentication | global `JwtAuthGuard` | inherited from the module, same as every sibling route |
17:| Role | `@Roles(UserRole.ORGANIZER, UserRole.ADMIN)` on the new route | an end player cannot reach it |
18:| Ownership | `tournamentAccessService.isManager(tournament, userId, systemRoles)` inside the service | the role alone is not enough; the caller must actually manage this tournament |
19:| Cross-tournament | `participant.tournamentId !== tournamentId` → 404 | prevents acting on a participant id from another tournament |
20:| Idempotency | early return when the flag already matches | no transaction, no audit, no broadcast; no write amplification from retries |
21:| Atomicity | one `db.transaction` around the refund request and the flag write | a failure can never leave the flag cleared with no refund recorded |
22:| Amount integrity | `calculateTournamentRefundQuote` computes the amount | the client has no field for it and no path to influence it |
23:| Double refund | `createPendingRefund` compare-and-sets `refund_status IS NULL`; `financial_ledger_entries.idempotency_key` is unique and not null | two independent barriers, one of them enforced by the database |
24:| Audit | `AuditService.logUpdate` on the participant row and, when a refund is requested, on the payment row | who flipped it and what it cost, recorded in the same transaction |
25:
26:## What was deliberately not added
27:
28:- No way to execute a refund. `confirm-refund` stays `@Roles(ADMIN)` and still
29:  requires a transaction proof URL.
30:- No client-supplied amount, reason enum, or participant status.
31:- No new table, column, enum value or migration, so there is no new attack surface
32:  on the data layer.
33:- No mass action. One row, one request, so a bulk mistake cannot happen.
34:
35:## Client-side
36:
37:- The checkbox is disabled while its own mutation is in flight, preventing a
38:  double-submit from one row.
39:- The confirmation text states that a refund request is being created and that the
40:  organizer still has to transfer the money back. It never quotes an amount,
41:  because the client cannot know it.
42:- Nothing sensitive is rendered that the organizer could not already see.
43:
44:## Residual risk
45:
46:An organizer can mark a fee paid without any proof, which is the intended override.
47:The audit row is the only record of who did it. If that is unacceptable for a
48:deployment, the follow-up is an optional note or receipt field on the manual mark —
49:deliberately not built here because no such column exists and inventing one was
50:out of scope.

